{"status":"ok","message-type":"work","message-version":"1.0.0","message":{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,22]],"date-time":"2026-09-22T03:49:17Z","timestamp":1790048957211,"version":"4.0.1"},"reference-count":12,"publisher":"Wiley","issue":"8","license":[{"start":{"date-parts":[[2008,5,2]],"date-time":"2008-05-02T00:00:00Z","timestamp":1209686400000},"content-version":"vor","delay-in-days":0,"URL":"http:\/\/onlinelibrary.wiley.com\/termsAndConditions#vor"}],"content-domain":{"domain":[],"crossmark-restriction":false},"short-container-title":["Microscopy Res &amp; Technique"],"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2008,8]]},"abstract":"<jats:title>Abstract<\/jats:title>\n                  <jats:p>\n                    Measurements of thickness using electron energy loss spectroscopy (EELS) are revised. Absolute thickness values can be quickly and accurately determined with the Kramers\u2010Kronig sum method. The EELS data analysis is even much easier with the log\u2010ratio method, however, absolute calibration of this method requires knowledge of the mean free path of inelastic electron scattering \u03bb. The latter has been measured here in a wide range of solids and a scaling law \u03bb \u223c \u03c1\n                    <jats:sup>\u22120.3<\/jats:sup>\n                    versus mass density \u03c1 has been revealed. EELS measurements critically depend on the excitation and collection angles. This dependence has been studied experimentally and theoretically and an efficient model has been formulated. Microsc. Res. Tech., 2008. \u00a9 2008 Wiley\u2010Liss, Inc.\n                  <\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1002\/jemt.20597","type":"journal-article","created":{"date-parts":[[2008,5,3]],"date-time":"2008-05-03T23:13:47Z","timestamp":1209856427000},"page":"626-631","source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":237,"title":["Thickness measurements with electron energy loss spectroscopy"],"prefix":"10.1002","volume":"71","author":[{"given":"K.","family":"Iakoubovskii","sequence"